{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P4iQ2XTFG7x846k9f6teri","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd016-dirname\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/dirname: Strip Last Component from File Paths\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  Shell scripts extract directory components from paths using dirname. The macOS BSD\n6\t  dirname processes only a single argument, while GNU dirname accepts multiple arguments\n7\t  and supports NUL-delimited output. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n8\t  reference binary (gdirname, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all argument\n9\t  combinations and edge cases, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n10\t\n11\tgoals:\n12\t  - G1: Strip the last component from one or more file paths, outputting the directory portion.\n13\t  - G2: Handle edge cases (root, trailing slashes, no directory component) per POSIX and GNU behavior.\n14\t  - G3: Support NUL-delimited output (-z) for safe handling of directory names containing newlines.\n15\t\n16\trequirements:\n17\t  R1:\n18\t    title: Core path stripping behavior\n19\t    items:\n20\t      - R1.1:\n21\t          text: Must strip trailing slashes from NAME, then remove the last component (everything after the final '/') and print the result.\n22\t      - R1.2:\n23\t          text: \"When NAME contains no '/' after trailing-slash removal, must output '.' (the current directory).\"\n24\t      - R1.3:\n25\t          text: \"When NAME is '/' or consists entirely of slashes, must output '/'.\"\n26\t      - R1.4:\n27\t          text: Must strip trailing slashes from the result. If the result after stripping would be empty, output the root slash instead.\n28\t      - R1.5:\n29\t          text: Must process multiple NAME arguments, printing one result per argument.\n30\t\n31\t  R2:\n32\t    title: Output formatting\n33\t    items:\n34\t      - R2.1:\n35\t          text: Each result must be followed by a newline character, or by a NUL byte (0x00) if -z or --zero is given.\n36\t      - R2.2:\n37\t          text: Results must be printed in the order the arguments are given, one per line.\n38\t\n39\t  R3:\n40\t    title: Exit codes and error handling\n41\t    items:\n42\t      - R3.1:\n43\t          text: Must exit 0 on success.\n44\t      - R3.2:\n45\t          text: Must exit 1 when called with no arguments. Must print an error message to stderr.\n46\t      - R3.3:\n47\t          text: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n48\t\n49\t  R4:\n50\t    title: Differential testing\n51\t    items:\n52\t      - R4.1:\n53\t          text: Differential tests must compare stdout output and exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary via pkg/testutils.\n54\t      - R4.2:\n55\t          text: \"Tests must cover: simple path (dir/file), nested path (a/b/c), trailing slashes, root path (/), relative path with no directory (file.txt -> '.'), dot path (.), double-dot path (..), multiple arguments, and NUL-delimited output (-z).\"\n56\t      - R4.3:\n57\t          text: Tests must verify error output and exit code when no arguments are given.\n58\t\n59\tnon_goals:\n60\t  - cmd/dirname does not resolve symlinks or canonicalize paths; it operates on the string representation only.\n61\t  - cmd/dirname does not read from stdin; all input comes from command-line arguments.\n62\t  - cmd/dirname does not handle encoding; paths are treated as byte sequences with '/' as the separator.\n63\t\n64\tacceptance_criteria:\n65\t  - id: AC1\n66\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/dirname /usr/bin/sort produces '/usr/bin', matching gdirname.\"\n67\t    traces:\n68\t      - R1.1\n69\t      - R1.2\n70\t      - R1.3\n71\t      - R1.4\n72\t      - R1.5\n73\t  - id: AC2\n74\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/dirname stdio.h produces '.', matching gdirname.\"\n75\t    traces:\n76\t      - R1.1\n77\t      - R1.2\n78\t      - R1.3\n79\t      - R1.4\n80\t      - R1.5\n81\t  - id: AC3\n82\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/dirname /usr/bin/ produces 